# The operator list for the reduced-operator ONNX Runtime build. ON by default
# (BB_ORT_REDUCED_OPS=1) since 2026-08-23; BB_ORT_REDUCED_OPS=0 builds the full
# set, which is how you bisect a suspected missing-operator failure.
#
# REGENERATE WITH scripts/gen-ort-ops-config.py, NEVER BY HAND -- the rows below
# are a union of three tools and hand-editing one silently breaks that property.
#
# Verified on an arm64 AVD (BB_Phone_16x9, api 34) on 2026-08-23, with this list
# and these models: 1/1 cases fully pass, 0 field assertions failed. All three
# model sessions are initialized in that run (BatchRunner passes
# useOrientationCls = true), which is what makes it a complete check -- a missing
# kernel throws at session init, so a scan that completes proves every node in
# all three graphs resolved one.
#
# ONNX Runtime can be compiled with only the operators a fixed set of models
# needs (`--include_ops_by_config`). For our three PP-OCRv5 graphs that is worth
# about 7.3 MB of the shipped .so. #32 enabled it and shipped a build whose
# session init died with
#
#     Could not find an implementation for <Op>(<n>) node with name '...'
#
# WHY A SINGLE TOOL CANNOT PRODUCE THIS LIST
#
# The operators a session needs are not the operators in the model file. ORT
# runs graph optimizations at session init (we set no level, so ORT_ENABLE_ALL)
# and those both CREATE and REMOVE operators. Neither generator describes the
# session we actually run:
#
#   - tools/python/create_reduced_build_config.py reads the raw .onnx, so it
#     misses everything the optimizer CREATES (FusedConv, FusedMatMul,
#     SkipLayerNormalization, QuickGelu). That was #32's first failure.
#   - onnxruntime.tools.convert_onnx_models_to_ort optimizes first, so it has
#     the fusions -- and DROPS what the optimizer folded away. The app then
#     failed on Pow(13), which the runtime had kept.
#
# THIS LIST IS THE UNION OF BOTH, plus a third list generated as a check.
# Measured 2026-08-23 against these exact models, ORT 1.24.2:
#
#   A  raw .onnx                                    27 ops
#   B  offline .ort conversion                      30 ops
#   C  runtime-optimized dump (see below)           30 ops
#   UNION                                           35 ops
#
#   A uniquely contributes:  Div, HardSwish, Identity, Pow, Sqrt
#   B uniquely contributes:  -
#   C uniquely contributes:  -
#
# So the offline conversion was short FIVE operators, not just the one Pow that
# #32 crashed on. Adding them one crash at a time would have taken five more
# ~4-minute rebuild + emulator cycles and still had no defined end.
#
# List C is ORT's own answer to "what graph do you actually build": a session
# opened at ORT_ENABLE_ALL on the CPU EP with SessionOptions.
# optimized_model_filepath set, then fed back through
# create_reduced_build_config.py. It contributed nothing beyond B, which is the
# useful part -- it means B's fusion set already matches what the runtime
# produces, so the union's only real additions are A's five folded-away ops.
#
# WHY THE UNION IS MORE THAN "CHEAP AND PROBABLY SAFER"
#
# It is closed under whether a fusion fires. Note that C, taken on a FULL build,
# folds Pow away -- yet the device (a REDUCED build) kept it and crashed. Two
# explanations fit, and we cannot yet separate them:
#
#   (a) op reduction changes which fusions fire, so a reduced build can be left
#       holding the pre-fusion operators that the optimized-graph list dropped.
#       If so, any list derived from an optimized graph is self-invalidating.
#   (b) that crash was on the OLD x1_0 textline model (#32 predates core
#       v0.10.0), and the current x0_25 graph would not have hit it.
#
# The union needs neither to be true: it carries both the pre-fusion and the
# post-fusion operators, so it is correct whichever way a fusion goes. That is
# why it is the route taken rather than "ship .ort models", which would be
# correct by construction but is a model-format change reaching beanbeaver-core,
# device_sim and desktop.
#
# The rows below are the tools' own output union'd per (domain, opset), so some
# ops appear at more than one opset. That is deliberate and harmless -- ORT
# resolves each to the kernel whose opset range covers it -- and it keeps the
# provenance of each row readable.
#
# TO REGENERATE (needs python onnxruntime pinned to the SAME version
# build-ort-android.sh builds, since which fusions fire is a property of that
# version -- read it from ort-sys's build/download/dist.txt, the `ms@<ver>`):
#
#   scripts/gen-ort-ops-config.py --models app/src/main/assets/models --out - 
#
# THE GATE -- nothing cheaper substitutes:
#
#     ./scripts/android-e2e.sh "$(./scripts/e2e-fixtures.sh)" --pilot
#
# against a real arm64 device or an arm64 AVD, which an Apple Silicon Mac runs
# natively. No host build can catch a missing operator -- the host links pyke's
# stock prebuilt, which has every one -- and no GitHub-hosted runner can either.
# Symbol inspection cannot catch it: reduction removes kernel *registrations*,
# not class definitions, so a broken build still shows ~50 FusedConv symbols.
# assert-ort-ops-config-fresh.sh cannot catch it either: it compares this file
# to the MODELS, not to the ENGINE, and printed green all through #32.
#
# Models this list was generated from (shasum -a 256 models/*.onnx):
#   8aad3208eac0bda67cb68a4c03cf376f25f9638764b6aa115a2bfa1815e76600  PP-LCNet_x0_25_textline_ori.onnx
#   d5de5df358366210d16419b9636a2fc1efa5d7a20688f38a7869ec7b1a4f4f7d  PP-OCRv5_mobile_det.onnx
#   20b6945179e9aa1dabe8770a0e73dba9a05350431549e58d7e88837f033c2890  PP-OCRv5_mobile_rec.onnx
#
ai.onnx;1;GlobalAveragePool,LayerNormalization,SimplifiedLayerNormalization
ai.onnx;6;HardSigmoid
ai.onnx;11;AveragePool,Conv,ConvTranspose
ai.onnx;13;Concat,Gemm,MatMul,ReduceMean,Resize,Shape,Sigmoid,Slice,Softmax,Split,Squeeze,Transpose,Unsqueeze
ai.onnx;14;Add,AveragePool,BatchNormalization,Concat,Conv,ConvTranspose,Div,Gemm,GlobalAveragePool,HardSigmoid,HardSwish,Identity,LayerNormalization,MatMul,Mul,Pow,ReduceMean,Relu,Reshape,Resize,Shape,Sigmoid,SimplifiedLayerNormalization,Slice,Softmax,Split,Sqrt,Squeeze,Sub,Transpose,Unsqueeze
com.microsoft;1;FusedConv,FusedMatMul,QuickGelu,SkipLayerNormalization
